<!-- ═══ IF YOU ARE THE ONE BEING CHECKED IN ON ════════════════════════════
     The callout below answered "what will you do with what I say" and never
     answered "why would I want this". Every word of it is defensive, correctly
     so, and defence was all it was. The reason to want it is that being asked
     separately is the fairest anybody has ever been asked: you do not have to
     be in the room, quick on your feet, or first to your manager's door.

     NOT "fairness improves team culture" - unprovable, the register every HR
     product already occupies, and not how anything else here talks. The
     mechanism is stated; the reader draws the conclusion. -->
<section class="fade art-bg" style="--ground:var(--gw-paper);background-image:url(/art/puzzle-three.png)">
  <div class="w">
    <p class="lab">If you are the one being checked in on</p>
    <h2 class="h2" style="font-size:25px">Everyone is asked the same way, at the same time.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:14px">Your account carries the same weight whether or not you were in the room, and whether or not you got to anybody first.</p>
    <p style="font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--gw-text);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 26px">Fairness here is not a value on a wall. It is how the questions get asked, and a team that trusts how it is asked argues about the work instead of about who said what.</p>

    <div class="flow">
      <div class="node"><b>You</b><span>your own check-in</span></div>
      <div class="node"><b>Them</b><span>their own check-in</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="down">&#8595;</div>
    <div class="one"><b>One shared picture</b><span>everybody in it is told the moment it is ready</span></div>

    <p style="font-size:15px;font-weight:700;margin:26px 0 0">Your answers do not go sideways.</p>
    <ul class="nots">
      <li><i>&#215;</i>Not to your manager, in your words.</li>
      <li><i>&#215;</i>Not to your colleagues.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- VERIFIED LINE BY LINE BEFORE IT WENT ON A PUBLIC PAGE, and carried over
         unchanged. "Never shares individual answers" was here once and was not
         true: a real report's divergences carry direct quotes. What is never
         shared is anybody's account of somebody ELSE, which is the promise
         people actually need. Two of these sentences are pinned by a guard that
         checks the site does not promise more than the product delivers. -->
    <div class="callout">
      <p>Nobody can speak for you. Your check-in is read and synthesized by a language model, not by a person: not by us, and not by whoever brought your team here. Leaders get the report. They cannot request an individual's check-in, and there is no screen anywhere that shows one, including ours.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

